On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:35:33 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Okay I like this. One note: the "Approved: once the doas question is
> settled" is a bad example. It's additional a condition to this, and the
> whole point is that it's deterministic. If there's an "Approved:"
> trailer, it's approved.

Agreed, and that example was wrong. The documentation in the series
below says so outright: Approved: means approved as-is, the value is
an optional note such as thanks or a follow-up idea, and it never adds
a condition.

> Let's do approved, superseded, rejected at the thread level for the time
> being. If I need to get more granular, I will.

Done. Approved: and Rejected: tag every message in the thread the
reply sits in, just as Superseded-by does.

> No, I'll manually filter them for the time being. Once it's
> demonstrating reliable value I can config notmuch to hide automatically.

Approved and rejected are not excluded. One question back: "[PATCH
3/3] notmuch: Hide superseded threads from searches" [1] still adds
superseded to search.exclude_tags in the sample config. If "them"
covers superseded too, I will drop that patch and send v2 of that
series.

> Can trailers be multiline? How does that work? I'm imagining that I
> might type a paragraph and it will word wrap.

Yes, if every continuation line starts with a space or a tab. git
interpret-trailers joins them into one value:

    Rejected: the hook belongs in the mail workspace, where
      the list is indexed

A line that wraps back to column 0 is not a trailer, though, and one
such line voids the whole block. That also drops any Superseded-by
in it. I checked both cases with git 2.56.0. So fill-paragraph with
no indent loses the verdict without a word. If you write a long reason,
either indent the continuation lines or put the paragraph in the prose
above and keep the trailer short.

> Fine if not... in any case, the whole trailer protocol needs to be
> documented.

The series is "[PATCH 0/2] notmuch: Tag approved and rejected
threads" [2]. Patch 1 adds the approved and rejected tags to
mail-tag.sh. The latest verdict on a thread wins, and within one
message the last trailer wins. Patch 2 adds a "Status trailers"
section to zib/patch-review-workflow.org. It says where trailers go,
what voids them, how values continue, and what each key means. It
builds on "[PATCH 0/3] notmuch: Tag superseded series and hide them"
[3] and on "[PATCH] notmuch: Tag patches reported committed" [4].

I tested it on a scratch maildir: a later rejection overrides an
earlier approval, quoted and badly wrapped trailers are ignored, and a
second run changes nothing. No message on the list has a verdict yet,
so applying it tags nothing until you write one.

